	Last May I decided to write a short little story, that was
very recursive and written mostly to impress Julie and partly for
fun.  I included it with some stories I sent Rose Moss and she made
some comments on it.  She found it interesting and referred to it in
conversations months later.  This made me think that maybe there was
something to it and it was not as silly as I had thought.  She also
remarked that the telling was confusing at places and had discovered
quite a large number of grammatical and other sentence level mistakes.
While I knew I should write without such mistakes, my ego was protected
by the praise for the ideas.
	Anyway on Wednesday night before class, I decided to work on the
story and hand it in the next day.  It would be fun and it would 
demonstrate to the class that I can write unusual kinds of stories.
I would be able to play with the readers, mention them by name, predict
their reactions, thus placing them in a quandary.  If they reacted as the
story indicated they would react, then they might feel manipulated.  
If, however, they intentionally react contrary to the story then they
will have been manipulated into behaving in a fashion different from what they
would have, had the story not predicted their actions.  Right now, while
they read this, they are probably thinking this is silly, the reasoning
is poor, "Why can't I just react to it as any other story and not pay any
special attention to the parts that refer to me?" they will wonder.
	Before I get on with the story I would like to present the 
original uncut (and therefore un-improved) version of the story:
.begin select 3
.skip 2 narrow 6,6 single space
.unlig
.begin center
A Recursive Visit to the Government of the Universe Store
by
Kenneth Kahn
on
May 5, 1975
dedicated to
Julie, Henry and Dan 
(and myself since I'm in it too)
.end
.skip 2
	Well it all started a couple of weeks ago.
Dan, Julie, Henry and myself were coming back from
some Bugs Bunny cartoons.  I happened to think of
the Government of the Universe Store and asked them
if they had ever heard of it.  They hadn't.  I told
them what I knew which wasn't much.
	There is this old building not far from Mass
Ave that has signs up indicating that it is a
Government of the Universe Store and sells (presumably
among other things) a universal communicator for $1.25.
They were interested but we were hungry (if I have this
right at all) and went to get ice cream and planned to go
someother time.  I think I do have this wrong since I
somehow remember a car during this conversation but who
cares about details.  We hypothesized as to what it might
be.  We realized that with high probability that it either
was a bunch of kooks or a rip-off scheme.  However I think
I made the comment that if this (this being reality) were
a Schekley story just this kind of place would indeed be
the real thing and ...
	A few days later we tried looking them up in
the telephone book----no go.  We thought of going the
following Sunday but we didn't quite get together besides
it was raining and late (I thought I remembered that they
close early).  Oh well.
	The next day I started to think about what if it
really were something other than a bunch of nuts or a hiest
(is that the right word?).  Anyway I thought it would be
fun (and maybe even funny) if I wrote a little imaginary story
about us going over there and it turning out to be more interesting
than we had imagined.  Problem was I couldn't think of what
should happen.  One thought was that the communicator really
worked and that the Government of the Universe wanted to
perform an experiment on us.  The experiment was designed to
see how humans would react to their innermost desires being
fulfilled.
Well by some strange coincidence the Government of the 
Universe decided that we all wanted to be aboard a star ship
exploring the universe and having fun.  So we all ended up 
on a highly sophisticated space ship controlled and piloted by
a rather friendly AI.  
	Then a great idea came to me.  I could write the
story starting off with the facts (as best as I could 
remember them) and then end up with a rather kooky visit.
Of course I would let the story recurse by mentioning within
the story my thinking about writing and then writing the
story and then showing it to Julie, Dan and Henry (and 
myself of course) and letting them recurse while they read
about them reading it...
	Anyway I wrote the story.  If you are interested
maybe I can find a copy of it.  The next day I did show
it to them and they reacted as you would of expected.  They
were disappointed in the section that talked about how they
reacted to the story since they were curious how
well the story predicted their reactions.
.next page
	After reading that story there was no doubt we had
to go and visit and be disappointed.  I had thought when I
was writing the story to give a strongly anti-climatic ending
and when we finally go, there is nothing of interest there and
we leave thinking we should never have gone and ruined a good
story.  But we did go and it was rather unusual.
	On the way there we were joking around about the story
and what if when we went the story turns out to be predictive and
the communicator really does work and we do go off on a space
ship with a friendly AI pilot.  Guess what folks? Thats
what really happened.  One day while we were exploring 
Andromeda's galactic center we talked about my story again
(of course it also was one of the first things we talked about after
we discovered ourselves aboard the starship).  I think it
was Julie (if it wasn't then it was either Dan, Henry,
myself or Irving (the name we gave to our AI friend and pilot)) that maybe the Government of the Universe inspired in a more
direct way than we thought the creation of my story.  "Perhaps",
she said (or he or thon), "the Government of the Universe wanted us
to know what was going to happen to test our reactions before it
tested us by doing this."  I replied, (or else Henry, Dan or
Irving (or Julie if she didn't say the first sentence))
"Maybe they are still doing it.  If they can make me write
stories maybe they can make you come up with the idea that
they made up the story for us to read".
	Sure it was a little confusing figuring out "the why"
of it
all (but then wasn't that always the case) but it was fun.  I
could tell you of the wonders we saw, of the weird races we
met up with, of the contents of the ship we were the only
passengers of, but I don't think i will spoil this story for you.
We have to leave a little bit out so we can have fun when we
go.  You wouldn't want to know everything that is going to 
happen, would we?
.end
.skip 2
	Now that you have read the original you'll have to suffer with
me as I revise it, partially in accordance with Rose Moss's comments
(she probably is a little annoyed with this stories attitude towards her
reaction to the original story but then again maybe she likes the role she plays in this)
and also to play with other members of the class as they read on.
	Ok to start with Rose Moss's first comment, an "I" above the word "%3myself%1."
I will give in here, because it looks bad to use poor grammar, despite the
intended affect of providing a recurring theme, namely, "Dan, Julie, Henry and 
myself" as in the dedication and as in the end of the fifth paragraph.  The 
second sentence now reads: "%3Dan, Julie, Henry and I were coming back
from some Bugs Bunny cartoons%1."
	Next I made a change which to mention here would bother my readers had
I not written this sentence that they are now reading.  The change was a correction of a
typo in the second paragraph, "%3someother%1" became "%3some other%1."  
Now any sensible reader
would have thought "Oh so what?  Why should I have to read about such trivialities?"  
Few readers, however, thought such thoughts because I wrote about their hypothetical
reaction to reading something without the context I am hereby providing.  In the 
future, however, I will only mention the correction and not the reaction to reading
about these kinds of corrections.
	Now for some real editing.  The phase "%3but who cares about details%1" was
underlined by Rose Moss and it does have to go.  
It now reads: "...%3 I somehow remember a
car during this conversation but details do not the story make%1."  The next two 
sentences were criticized for the tone created by the phrases "%3hypothesized as to%1"
and "%3with high probability%1."  The tone is appropriate for the MIT-ish group that 
was involved ("Henry, Dan, Julie and myself").  But the word "%3realized%1"
does not go well with the phrase "%3with high probability%1" so it now reads:
"%3We concluded that, with high probability, it was either %1..."
	The word "%3them%1" in the third paragraph was pointed out as ambiguous, it is
now replaced with "%3the store%1." 
	The last word of the fourth paragraph perhaps needs some explanation 
since Rose Moss, Anne, Patty, Marjorie, and Shelley aren't from an
environment where AI is a household word (it is for Julie and Henry, of course,
and maybe even for Dan too).  AI, or its less abbreviated form "Artificial
Intelligence," in the context of the last sentence of the fourth paragraph
means an intelligent machine.  A footnote might remedy the problem so imagine
a little "%31%1" at the end there and at the bottom of the page the following text:
.begin indent 0,0 unlig
.skip select 3 single space
1. AI is an abbreviation for Artificial Intelligence which in this context is a
label for intelligent machines.
.end 
	"%3Would of%1" in paragraph six should of been "would have" (yes I know, the
"should of" in this sentence should of have been "should have").  They don't
sound very different when spoken out loud, so that is the excuse I shall put
forth to protect the readers' image of this writer.  
	I apologize for the lack of clarity in the sentence that follows the last
one that I just edited but I'm not going to change it.  Even though that sounds
like a childish attitude to take, I am not afraid that readers will think me 
childish since I am aware of the possibility, while children are not typically
credited with being so aware of themselves.  Likewise for the strange switching 
of tenses in the next paragraph.
	Wonder what Freud would have said about "%3anti-climatic%1"?
	To those readers that find reading about the editing of a story dull, despite
the meta-descriptions that accompany this section: cheer up, there are only two
paragraphs left of the original story.
	The first sentence of the next paragraph is lacking the word "wondered."
In accordance with a standard MIT joke I shall leave the placement of the word
as "an exercise to the reader."  
	I am a bit ashamed that I had written "%3Guess what folks?%1" so scratch that
sentence and start the next one off with the word "%3Well%1."
	I left out the word "%3said%1" after the double right parentheses----sorry.
"%3Thon%1" is the long sought after singular third person neuter animate pronoun
that was invented fifty years ago and never caught on for some reason that evades me.
	I don't love the last line and at least would change the "%3we%1" to "%3you%1." 
It was "%3we%1" because that word's referent was "Dan, Henry, Julie and myself."
.skip 3
	Well I didn't just edit the story and xerox it and hand it in at class instead
I handed this entire story in, but of course you (the letters "y," "o," and "u,"
in this case refer to "Patti, Anne, Marjorie, Rose Moss and Shelley) know that 
already.  What you don't know is what happened when it was discussed in class, since
you are reading it now, and will discuss it on December fourth.  You may object that
not only don't I know that it will be discussed on the fourth, but that it may be the
case that you are reading this after it has been discussed in class, perhaps years
later.  You will not be able to say, however, that this silly story made false
predictions, because it is hereby predicting that you will think it made false
predictions.  But it either has to make at least one correct prediction or else
you are not paying attention.
	Back to fiction, away from paradoxes.  We discussed Shelley's story first
and since this story is about my story I will not write further about that 
story.  First off, I should mention that Rose Moss was trapped, if she discussed
my story at a different point (or not at all) then she would have passed up a
great opportunity to let this story be self-fulfilling.  If, however, she felt
manipulated, if she desired to express her "free will" (of course she would be
expressing her "free will" equally well by deciding to discuss this story after
Shelley's), then she may discuss some other story after Shelley's.  (I could
insure my ability to prophecy by writing that she really discussed Patti's story
after Shelley's and then later saying that she really discussed Anne's story and so on.
This is called the "shotgun" approach to prediction.)
	So Rose Moss asked the class what they thought of this story.  Marjorie
was about to say she liked the idea, the way the story manipulates its readers,
when smiles broke out on everybody and a few laughs were heard.   Patti and Anne
felt it strange to be sitting there acting out the story they were about to
discuss.  The ridiculousness of the situation was at its peak at the very beginning
of the discussion, but once Shelley broke the "ice" by saying, "it says I should
start talking, so I may as well, but I'm not going to say what it said I should 
start off saying since its not what I'm thinking."
	Well they eventually broke through the initial barrier and started talking
about their reactions to the story and not what the story said they would say.
One  of the class members said, "I felt left out, I wondered why he forgot to include me
when all the others were mentioned by name."  That was Mimi.
.skip 3
.begin center
This story was dedicated to:

Rose Moss, Marjorie, Mimi, Anne, Shelley, Patti, myself

and the story itself (since its in it too)
.end

